# Captured originals are addressed by the sha256 of their exact bytes, so git must store them
# byte for byte. A CRLF page fetched on Windows is otherwise normalised on the way in and its
# stored content no longer hashes to its own file name.
#
# Each capture directory already carries its own `.gitattributes` saying `* -text`, and that
# stays the explicit form. This is the backstop for the failure that actually happened: a new
# `evidence-*` directory was added without one, and two PBOC pages were normalised before
# anyone noticed. Directories outside that naming convention still need their own file.
.scratch/**/evidence-*/** -text
